WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.090
Film Courage: Ric, SHOT CALLER explores the
cost of trying to survive in prison.

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.440
If we look at everything in life, everything
has a price.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.230
Sometimes it’s simply time versus money.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.639
What are the costs of being a screenwriter?

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:17.610
I realize the stakes aren’t as high as being
in the prison system, but what are the costs

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.710
of being a screenwriter?

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:23.859
Ric Roman Waugh: Hopefully the cost of me
being a screenwriter (at least for me) is

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:24.920
being authentic.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.520
You know the movies that I try to portray
is “Do I entertain you?”

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.520
Heck yeah.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.980
If you are going to pay money to sit in a
seat and watch my movie, I want you to be

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:34.980
entertained.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.010
I want you to be thrilled, but I also hope
that you are getting something out of it.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:43.600
To where it’s not so much about the message
of the movie, but it makes you think about

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:44.600
it.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.760
It makes you think about your own life and
see the movie in a way that makes it feel

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:53.000
like it’s so grounded and authentic that
this could be us navigating this world and

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.780
this world is very real with what we are seeing.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:02.280
So to me that is probably the scariest part
is all three of my movies FELON, SNITCH and

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:07.270
SHOT CALLER, the first thing that I showed
this to were law enforcement and gang members.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.180
Because it either lives or dies there, right?

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.160
This is either authentic to them saying that
yeah I know that these three stories are fiction

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:17.800
(well, SNITCH is a true story), but if you
look at these three things and say yeah most

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.920
of these characters in the world are fictional
but it’s a 100% accurate of what this is.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.830
This is our world that we live every single
day.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.320
So that’s the type of validation I am looking
for in the stories that I tell.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.990
Film Courage: In terms of screenplay structure,
is there a certain format that you follow

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:35.990
when you start writing?

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:36.990
Ric Roman Waugh: Yeah…I don’t.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.900
I don’t follow format.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:46.780
I think that for me I think a lot of screenwriters
now and a lot of filmmakers are getting too

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.360
caught up with the structure of things and
I like to kind of mess that up.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:55.330
I like to be unconventional and I like to
think ‘If I’m sitting here watching this

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:59.790
movie, am I moved forward at every single
moment?

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:04.090
Am I learning something new, am I sucked into
this world, whether the first act break came

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.670
at page 30 or 70.’

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:10.600
Whatever it may be, it’s this thing that
propels itself and moves it forward.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.930
So for me it’s about storytelling first
and formula and structure second.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.150
Film Courage: So there are not certain beats
that you’re trying to hit at different points?

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:22.100
Ric Roman Waugh: Yeah…I think when you’ve
written as many things as I’ve written at

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:27.430
this point in my life, it all comes kind of
intuitively or instinctively?

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:31.270
You know where these things are going to be
whether it is you where this movie is going

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.730
to go in the first 10 minutes, it’s not
meandering and you’re sitting in the theater

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.730
for 30 minutes going ‘What the hell is this
movie going to be about?”

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:42.580
So yes, there are certain bullet points that
I’m trying to hit throughout it, but at

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.600
this point it’s just extinctive to good
storytelling.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.650
At least I hope it is.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.320
That is what my intentions are.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.220
Film Courage: So SHOT CALLER is your fourth
script?

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:56.740
Ric Roman Waugh: Fourth…no, I’ve written
countless others.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:04.230
Film Courage: And that’s since 2001 you’ve
been writing or earlier?

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.910
How many script would you say you’ve written,
even shorts, even something in a drawer that

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.069
no one will ever see?

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:14.400
Ric Roman Waugh: Between all of the studio
movies and scripts I’ve written, over 50

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:24.090
(as far as jobs), things that were either
produced or payed for or developed, at least

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.110
over 50.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:31.490
Film Courage: Your process for writing SHOT
CALLER, you became a prison volunteer?

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:36.220
Ric Roman Waugh: I became a volunteer with
the California Department of Corrections working

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:42.380
with parole to where it could give me an unflinching
look at the prison system without somebody

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:46.560
knowing I was a filmmaker and trying to be
bias or protective or holding back.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.840
I got a candid look at the prison system from
the inside out.

